{
  "dom_id": "step-q36994",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y+{z}^{2}$ that passes through the point $P(1,0,1).$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,1) & =1\\cdot {0}^{2}+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0+{1}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}y+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,0,1)$ is the surface $z=\\pm \\sqrt{1-x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
